--TEST--
Core\Path reads the empty path as the current directory's contents — its parent is `.` and it normalizes to `.` — where PHP's dirname answers the empty string, which is the one input on which PHP hands back something no caller can use
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`dirname('')` is `'.'` here and `''` in PHP (`path.rs:37`, which is the one home of why): the empty path names the current directory's contents, so its parent is the current directory, and PHP's answer is the one input where `dirname` hands back a string that is not a usable directory at all — concatenating it with a separator produces an absolute path, which is the opposite of what the caller asked for. Everything else § 8 answers about the empty path agrees with PHP where PHP has an answer: `basename('')` is `''` on both sides, `pathinfo('')` sets no extension key and this answers `null`, and `realpath('')` is a disk question no member here asks. The remaining rows have no PHP twin to diverge from — `split`, `join`, `isAbsolute` and `relativeTo` are members PHP does not have — and are here because the empty path is the input each of them is most likely to have been written without.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// One subject, asked of every member of § 8 that takes a path, because the
// empty path is the input a decomposition is least likely to have been written
// for and the one where an off-by-one in a scan first shows.
var $empty = "";

// The divergence itself, and the reading behind it: the parent of "everything
// in the current directory" is the current directory.
echo "[", Core\Path::dirname($empty), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Path::dirname($empty, {levels: 3}), "]\n";

// What PHP agrees with. A root is not an absent name and neither is the empty
// path, so `basename` answers the empty string rather than null, and an
// extension that is not there is `null` (`rule:core-api/shape-rules` R5) where `pathinfo` leaves
// its key out.
echo "[", Core\Path::basename($empty), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Path::basename($empty, {withoutExtension: true}), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Path::extension($empty) ?? "<null>", "]\n";

// Normalizing agrees with the parent above: the path that names the current
// directory's contents is the current directory, spelled the one way § 8
// spells it.
echo "[", Core\Path::normalize($empty), "]\n";

// The empty path holds no components, so it splits to no components — not to
// one empty one, which is what `explode` over a separator would answer and is
// the shape that makes a later `join` produce a leading separator.
var $parts = Core\Path::split($empty);
echo Core\Arr::count($parts), "\n";

// And it is not absolute, is the identity of `join` on either side, and is a
// base that leaves a path as it was — while relating it to itself answers the
// same `.` the parent and the normalization did, which is what says the three
// readings are one reading.
echo Core\Path::isAbsolute($empty) ? "absolute" : "relative", "\n";
echo "[", Core\Str::replace(Core\Path::join($empty, "a", "b"), Core\Path::SEPARATOR, "/"), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Str::replace(Core\Path::join("a", $empty), Core\Path::SEPARATOR, "/"), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Path::relativeTo($empty, $empty) ?? "<null>", "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Str::replace(Core\Path::relativeTo("a/b", $empty) ?? "<null>", Core\Path::SEPARATOR, "/"), "]\n";
--EXPECT--
[.]
[.]
[]
[]
[<null>]
[.]
0
relative
[a/b]
[a]
[.]
[a/b]
